Q: ExpectedExceptionAttribute is not working in MSTest A: <p>Try the new way</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>Assert.Throws&lt;ArgumentNullException&gt;(() =&gt;
        throw new ArgumentNullException(&quot;test&quot;);
    );
</code></pre>

Q: How to send a silent Push Notification payload A: <p>If you send the payload like this:</p>
<pre><code>{
  &quot;content-available&quot;: 1
}
</code></pre>
<p>It will wake up the app in the background (silent push) and trigger the JavaScript handler, but it <strong>will not display any notification alert</strong> to the user.</p>
<p>If you want to display a notification (or handle it manually), you need to include the proper APS payload. For example:</p>
<pre><code>{
  &quot;aps&quot;: {
    &quot;badge&quot;: 1,
    &quot;content-available&quot;: 1
  },
  &quot;data&quot;: {
    &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Game Request&quot;,
    &quot;body&quot;: &quot;Bob wants to play poker&quot;,
  }
}
</code></pre>
<p>With this structure:</p>
<p>- &quot;content-available&quot;: 1 wakes up the app in the background.<br />
- &quot;badge&quot;: 1 updates the app icon badge.<br />
- The data object can be used to create a custom local notification inside your app.<br />
- You can choose to display a custom notification or suppress it based on your app logic.</p>
